<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!--
  O-Octagon Phase 4.2 — CR-b fixture.  PLAN-4.2 P106, D20.

  ═══ WHAT THIS FILE IS FOR ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  CR-a bounces through the SHIPPED DEFAULT venue and records which bounce channel carries tone k.
  On its own that measures Logic's canonical interleaved 7.1 order — and nothing else.  It cannot
  tell a map that is consulted from a map that is ignored, because under the default venue the map
  IS the identity (VenueModel.cpp:87-89).

  CR-b bounces the same project through THIS venue.  Every speaker's label moves, so if the map is
  actually consulted the whole bounce order shifts by one; if it is ignored, the result is
  indistinguishable from CR-a and the test fails.

  ═══ WHY AN 8-CYCLE AND NOT A SWAP ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  A two-element swap leaves six speakers on the identity, so six of the eight columns would prove
  nothing — D20's vacuity at one-quarter scale.  This is a DERANGEMENT: every speaker moves.

    speaker   default label (CR-a)   CR-b label   buffer idx   CR-b bounce channel
      1              L                   R             1               2
      2              R                   C             2               3
      3              C                   Lfe           3               4
      4              Lfe                 Lss           4               5
      5              Lss                 Rss           5               6
      6              Rss                 Lrs           6               7
      7              Lrs                 Rrs           7               8
      8              Rrs                 L             0               1

  Buffer indices are derived from ASCENDING ENUM-BIT ORDER, not from declaration order
  (critical_audiochannelset_is_a_bitset_not_an_order; ChannelMap.cpp:105-151 asserts it).

    analyse_bounce.py  with  mode=order  label=CR-b  expect=2,3,4,5,6,7,8,1
    (spelled with the usual double-dash flags on the command line; XML comments cannot contain a
    double dash, which is the only reason they are written as key=value here)

  The analyser REFUSES an identity expectation on a CR-b label (P105 clause 2 / N13), so a run
  against a venue that silently failed to load cannot report a clean CR-a pass.

  ═══ WHY ONLY @index AND @label APPEAR ON EACH SPEAKER ═══════════════════════════════════════════
  P106 requires this venue be "otherwise identical to the shipped default venue".  Transcribing the
  eight default positions here would make that identity a TRANSCRIPTION, and a transcription drifts
  the first time a default moves — silently, while still loading and still passing
  (pattern_test_fixture_mirrors_drift_silently).

  So the positions, trims and rake are OMITTED.  VenueFile::load reads into a fresh model that has
  already run resetToDefaults(), and every attribute the file does not carry falls back to that
  known default (VenueModel.cpp:194-197 and the per-attribute readFloat/readString fallbacks at
  :216-241).  The identity is therefore BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by copying, and it survives any
  future change to the default geometry.

  Speakers are located by the @index attribute, not by child position (VenueModel.cpp:219-221), so
  the eight children below are unambiguous.

  All eight labels are members of create7point1(), so buildSpeakerToBuffer cannot fail
  `labelNotInSet` on this file — the permutation is a re-assignment WITHIN the set, not a new set.
  Verify that claim the cheap way before using it: load this file in Standalone and confirm the
  status line reads mapInvalid == false (PLAN-4.2 Task 5).

  @name is deliberately NOT the default name: it is what tells the operator at a glance that all
  eight instances actually took the fixture.  The name is not part of the geometry or the map and
  does not enter any measurement.
-->

<VENUE name="CR-b permuted (Phase 4.2 fixture - NOT a measured venue)"
       schemaVersion="1">
  <SPEAKER index="1" label="R"/>
  <SPEAKER index="2" label="C"/>
  <SPEAKER index="3" label="Lfe"/>
  <SPEAKER index="4" label="Lss"/>
  <SPEAKER index="5" label="Rss"/>
  <SPEAKER index="6" label="Lrs"/>
  <SPEAKER index="7" label="Rrs"/>
  <SPEAKER index="8" label="L"/>
</VENUE>
